    "name":  "Drug-induced erectile dysfunction",
    "module":  "male-genital",
    "category":  "disease",
    "description":  "Drug-induced erectile dysfunction (ED) occurs when a medication impairs the physiological mechanisms required for penile erection — primarily by interfering with nitric oxide-mediated smooth muscle relaxation, altering hormone levels, or causing autonomic neuropathy. Common causative classes include antihypertensives (beta-blockers, thiazide diuretics), antidepressants (SSRIs, TCAs, MAOIs), antipsychotics, anti-androgens (spironolactone, finasteride), and opioids. The relationship is typically dose-dependent and reversible on drug withdrawal or substitution. A thorough medication review is a mandatory first step in evaluating ED.",
